In `WP_REST_Comments_Controller`, the `update_item()` method returns HTTP
  403 (Forbidden) when an invalid post ID is provided, but the correct status
  code is 404 (Not Found).

  The same file returns 404 for the identical scenario in `get_item()`
  (line 418), making this inconsistent within the same controller.

  403 implies the client lacks permission. 404 is correct here because
  the post does not exist — it is a missing resource, not an authorization
  failure.

  == Affected code (class-wp-rest-comments-controller.php) ==
     
{{{
if ( empty( $post ) ) {
    return new WP_Error(
        'rest_comment_invalid_post_id',
        __( 'Invalid post ID.' ),
        array( 'status' => 403 )  // incorrect
    );
}
}}}


  == Proposed Fix ==

{{{
array( 'status' => 404 )
}}}

It looks like these three constants are not used anymore:

{{{
define( ‘COMPRESS_CSS’, true );
define( ‘COMPRESS_SCRIPTS’, true );
define( ‘ENFORCE_GZIP’, true )
}}}

In #44815 the check for the "c" parameter (for compression) in load-scripts.php and load-styles.php was removed.

But it is still used in this file:
https://github.com/WordPress/WordPress/blob/master/wp-includes/script-loader.php#L2199
and
https://github.com/WordPress/WordPress/blob/master/wp-includes/script-loader.php#L2377

And documented at the beginning of the file:
https://github.com/WordPress/WordPress/blob/master/wp-includes/script-loader.php#L8-L10

It looks like we can safely remove this or is there a reason to keep it?

Follow-up to #63017

There are three constants for enabling PHP-based compression, which are not used anymore:

{{{
define( ‘COMPRESS_CSS’, true );
define( ‘COMPRESS_SCRIPTS’, true );
define( ‘ENFORCE_GZIP’, true )
}}}

Therefore, we should also remove them from the Site Health screen or maybe start with marking them as ''deprecated''.

It looks like we are not showing `ENFORCE_GZIP`, but the other two:

https://github.com/WordPress/WordPress/blob/de3286bd085400ca657d59c8c4e76632bd5591a8/wp-admin/includes/class-wp-debug-data.php#L1519-L1535

We could **not** show them if the constant is ''undefined''.

If the constant ''is'' defined, we could show the value and add a notice that this doesn't do anything anymore.

WordPress: 7.0-beta1
php 8.5
opcache enabled (file)

The site health checks the existence of opcache in includes/class-wp-site-health.php:

{{{
2803: if ( function_exists( 'opcache_get_status' ) )
}}}

But opcache_get_status "will not return any information about the file cache". It returns false and site health reports "Opcode cacheDisabled".
See https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.opcache-get-status.php

So WordPress incorrectly reports the existence of opcache.

----

''Analysis by Claude Opus 4.8:''

WordPress 7.0 introduces a new Site Health test for the PHP opcode cache (`WP_Site_Health::get_test_opcode_cache()`, added in r61612). The test detects whether OPcache is active by calling `opcache_get_status()`:

{{{#!php
<?php
public function get_test_opcode_cache(): array {
    $opcode_cache_enabled = false;
    if ( function_exists( 'opcache_get_status' ) ) {
        $status = @opcache_get_status( false );
        if ( $status && true === $status['opcache_enabled'] ) {
            $opcode_cache_enabled = true;
        }
    }
    // ...
}
}}}

The problem is that `opcache_get_status()` only reports on the **shared memory** cache. When OPcache is configured to use the **file cache** — in particular `opcache.file_cache_only=1`, where shared memory is unavailable or disabled — `opcache_get_status()` returns `false` even though opcode caching is fully active.

As the [https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.opcache-get-status.php PHP manual] notes, `opcache_get_status()` will not return any information about the file cache.

As a result, on sites where OPcache is running in file-cache-only mode, Site Health incorrectly reports **"Opcode cache is not enabled"**, a false negative.

**Steps to reproduce**
1. Run PHP with OPcache configured for file caching only, e.g. `opcache.enable=1`, `opcache.file_cache=/path/to/cache`, `opcache.file_cache_only=1`.
2. Visit **Tools → Site Health**.
3. Observe that the opcode cache test reports "Opcode cache is not enabled," despite OPcache being active.

**Proposed direction**

Detection should not rely solely on `opcache_get_status()`. It can fall back to checking whether the Zend OPcache extension is loaded and `opcache.enable` is on, treating a valid `opcache_get_status()` result as authoritative only when one is available.

When Ghostscript (`gs`) is not installed, running the "Media Handling" section of Site Health from the command line prints an error message coming from the shell:
{{{
sh: gs: not found
}}}
The "Ghostscript version" field is still correctly reported as "Not available", so nothing breaks. The output is only noisy.

== Cause ==
`WP_Debug_Data::get_wp_media()` runs `gs --version` through `exec()`:
{{{#!php
<?php
$gs = exec( 'gs --version' );
}}}

`exec()` only captures stdout. When `gs` does not exist, the shell (not PHP) writes `sh: gs: not found` to stderr, and a terminal shows it as is. The empty result is already handled just below (`empty( $gs )`), so the intent of the code is to report "Not available" quietly.
This probably goes unnoticed in a web request, where the stderr of the child process is not part of the page output.
This code has been there since this class was introduced in WordPress 5.2.

== Steps to reproduce ==
1. Use an environment where `gs` is not installed (`command -v gs` prints nothing).
2. Use a WP-CLI version that includes the `site-health` command. It is missing in WP-CLI 2.12.0 (stable), so I used the nightly build.
3. Run `wp site-health info wp-media`.
4. Observe `sh: gs: not found` printed above the table.

The same message appears in any command line context that calls `WP_Debug_Data::debug_data()`. I first noticed it while running the PHPUnit tests of a plugin.

'''Expected result:''' no output besides the table, and "Ghostscript version" shows "Not available".

'''Actual result:''' `sh: gs: not found` is printed on stderr.

Tested with: WordPress trunk (7.2.0 in `package.json`, commit c23817e), WP-CLI 3.0.0-alpha-dd11f5a (nightly), PHP 8.3.33, Docker (Linux), using the wordpress-develop local environment.

== Reproducing with PHPUnit ==
Save the following as `tests/phpunit/tests/admin/wpDebugData.php` and run `npm run test:php -- --filter Tests_Admin_wpDebugData`.
With the current code, the PHPUnit output contains `sh: 1: gs: not found` (the exact wording depends on the shell). With the proposed fix below, the output is clean and the test still passes.

{{{#!php
<?php
<?php
/**
 * @group admin
 * @group site-health
 */
class Tests_Admin_wpDebugData extends WP_UnitTestCase {

	public function test_debug_data_without_ghostscript() {
		require_once ABSPATH . 'wp-admin/includes/class-wp-debug-data.php';

		// Block the external request made by WP_Debug_Data.
		add_filter(
			'pre_http_request',
			function () {
				return new WP_Error( 'test_http_request', 'HTTP request disabled for this test.' );
			}
		);

		// Simulate "gs is not installed" with a PATH that does not contain it.
		$original_path = getenv( 'PATH' );
		putenv( 'PATH=/nonexistent' );

		try {
			$info = WP_Debug_Data::debug_data();
		} finally {
			putenv( 'PATH=' . $original_path );
		}

		// The test passes, but with the current code "sh: gs: not found" is printed.
		$this->assertSame(
			'not available',
			$info['wp-media']['fields']['ghostscript_version']['debug']
		);
	}
}
}}}


== Proposed fix ==

Check that `gs` exists before running it:


{{{#!php
<?php
$gs = exec( 'command -v gs >/dev/null && gs --version' );
}}}

`command -v` is a shell builtin specified by POSIX, whereas `which` is an external program that is not guaranteed to be installed. With this change, in my test environment, the message disappears and the field is still reported as "Not available".


== Notes ==

* The `wp-cli/site-health-command` package already works around this message in several of its acceptance tests (see the comments in `features/site-health.feature`: https://github.com/wp-cli/site-health-command/blob/main/features/site-health.feature), so other projects are affected too.
* As far as I can see, no existing core PHPUnit test covers this section of `WP_Debug_Data`. Since the message comes from a child process and not from PHP, I am not sure how to assert on it in an automated test. Suggestions are welcome.
* I will open a pull request with this change.
* Related: #61648 (where `get_wp_media()` was extracted; the `exec()` call itself is older and was only moved).

AI assistance: Claude (Anthropic) helped me understand the problem, look up the affected version and write this report.

Patch proposed by Chouby.

Backport for https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/pull/81160


== What?

Part of https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/issues/34198
This PR adds elements support to the label element.

== Why?
So a theme can consistently style how form labels look across the site, regardless of the blocks or third party plugins used. This continues the form elements work recently done for inputs and selects.

== How?
By allowing styling of label using the elements API

== Testing Instructions
1. Add the following to theme.json file:

{{{
"styles": {
		"elements": {
			"label": {
				"color": {
					"text": "red",
					"background": "blue"
				},
				"spacing": {
					"padding": {
						"top": "25px",
						"bottom": "25px",
						"left": "25px",
						"right": "25px"
					}
				}
			}
		}
	},
}}}

2. Insert a Custom HTML block with <label>Test Label</label> and verify the colors applied correctly in the editor and on the frontend.

